[Line "English Attack: 7...Be7 8.f3 Be6 9.Qd2 h5, the main line with 10.Be2"]
[Section "6.Be3 e5: the ...h5 set-up"]
[Summary "Black stops g4 before it starts, and the English Attack turns from a pawn race into a manoeuvring game. White trades on d5, Black ends up with the bishop pair and a comfortable open game."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 { [%cal Gf2f3,Gd1d2,Ge1c1,Gg2g4,Gh2h4] The English
Attack, today White's most popular sixth move. The bishop defends d4 for good, so the knight can be challenged
without any pin, and f3, Qd2, long castling and the g4-g5 and h4 pawn storm follow. It is a race: White's
pawns against the black king, Black's queenside pawns against the white king on the other wing. } 6... e5 {
[%cal Ge5d4] [%csl Yd5,Rd6] The principled reply. The pawn kicks the d4 knight, takes space and opens the f8
bishop; the price is the structure from the overview, a hole on d5 and a backward pawn on d6. The Scheveningen
set-up 6...e6 (sixth file) keeps d5 under control but gives White a free hand with g4, and 6...Ng4 (also
sixth file) is the drawing line. } 7. Nb3 { [%cal Gd4b3] The knight leaves the centre without blocking the
f-pawn or the bishop. 7.Nf3 (fifth file) is the quiet alternative. } ( 7. Nf5? { Hits d6 and g7 but loses
time. } 7... d5 8. Bg5 d4 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nb1 Bxf5 11. exf5 { Black has taken the centre with ...d5 and ...d4,
and White's pieces have been pushed back. } ) ( 7. Nde2 Be6 8. f4 Nbd7 { The knight on e2 blocks the bishop
and the queen, and Black develops with ease. } ) 7... Be7 { [%cal Rf2f4] The accurate move order. 7...Be6 is
the more common move, but it allows 8.f4. The text avoids that, and after 8.f3 Be6 the main line is reached
either way. } 8. f3 { [%cal Gg2g4,Gd1d2] Defends e4 so that the c3 knight is free, takes g4 from Black's knight
and prepares the pawn storm. } 8... Be6 { [%csl Yd5,Yc4] The bishop guards d5 and hits the knight on b3 and c4.
It is safe from f4, because White has already used a tempo on f3. } 9. Qd2 { [%cal Ge1c1] Prepares long
castling and covers d5: if Black plays ...d5 now, White castles long and has the d-file. } ( 9. g4 { Too early. }
9... d5! { [%cal Gd5e4] The answer to an early g4 in every English Attack: the centre opens while White's king is still in the middle. } 10. g5 ( 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 Bxd5 { Black has the stronger centre and active
bishops. } ) 10... d4 11. gxf6 Bxf6 { Black regains the piece with a better position. } ) 9... h5!? { [%csl Rg4]
The modern idea, used at the top level. It stops g4 for good, because g4 hxg4 fxg4 would open the h-file for
Black. The old answer 9...O-O leads to the pawn race of the first game of the next file; with ...h5 White cannot attack
with the g-pawn, so the game becomes positional. The cost is that castling short is no longer attractive, so
the king stays in the middle for a while. } 10. Be2 { Flexible: White waits to see where Black puts his king,
and prepares kingside castling. 10.Nd5 and 10.O-O-O have their own games. } 10... Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7b6,Gd7c5] }
11. Nd5 { [%csl Yc6,Yd5] Now that the b8 knight has gone to d7, nothing guards c6. White plans exchanges on d5,
exd5 and Na5-c6. } 11... Nxd5 12. exd5 Bf5 { [%cal Gf5c2] The bishop leaves the attacked square and hits c2. }
13. O-O O-O 14. Na5 { [%csl Yc6,Rb7] } 14... Qb8 { [%cal Gb8d6] The study's choice; elite games mostly play 14...Qc7 (or 14...Qc8), but then the
knight can sit on a5 for ever. With the queen on b8 the bishop can go to d8 and remove it, and the queen also guards d6.
14...Qc8 looks active, but 15.c3 Bd8 16.Nc4 Qb8 gives White a pressure on d6 that the text avoids. } 15. c4 {
[%cal Gc4c5,Gb2b4] } 15... Bd8 { [%cal Gd8a5] Forces the knight to decide: Nb3 would block White's own b-pawn,
and b4 is met by ...Bxa5. } 16. Rac1 Bg6 17. Bd1 { [%cal Gd1a4,Rd7c5] Ba4 would challenge the knight on d7,
which guards c5. } 17... Bxa5 18. Qxa5 b5! { [%cal Gb5c4] Challenges the c4 pawn at once. If it is taken,
...axb5 and ...Rxa2 hits a pawn. } 19. Qd2 { **Verdict:** equal and comfortable for Black. The queenside is
open for ...a5, ...Rc8 and ...Nc5 ideas, White's d5 pawn is a target and the black bishop on g6 eyes the
whole diagonal. } *

[Line "English Attack: 9...h5 10.O-O-O Nbd7 11.Kb1 b5"]
[Section "6.Be3 e5: the ...h5 set-up"]
[Summary "White castles long and waits with Kb1, and Black expands on the queenside. The critical continuations are 12.Nd5 for a quick ending, 12.h3 and 12.f4."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. f3 Be6 9. Qd2 h5 10. O-O-O { White
castles long, as in the old main line, but g4 is not available. } 10... Nbd7 { Black does not castle: with the
h-pawn on h5 the king is better in the centre. } 11. Kb1 { [%cal Gc1b1] The usual waiting move. It takes the king
off the c-file, where ...Bxb3 and ...Qa5 ideas would be annoying, and keeps White flexible. } 11... b5 { [%cal
Gb5b4,Gd7b6,Gd8c7] Black expands. The general plan is ...Qc7, ...Nb6 and ...b4 or ...Nc4; the knight on b6 also
guards d5. } 12. Nd5 { The most forcing: White trades off the knight on d5 and heads for a quiet position. } (
12. h3 { Covers g4 so that f4 or g4 becomes possible. } 12... h4 { Stops g4. } 13. Bd3 Qc7 14. f4 Nb6 { Black
has a good game: the queen guards e5 and the knight eyes c4 and a4. } ) ( 12. g3 { A slower way to play for g4:
it stops ...h4, and h3 and g4 follow. } 12... Qc7 13. h3 Nb6 14. Rg1 b4 15. Ne2 Nc4 { Black is much better:
White spent too much time on the pawn moves and Black's counterplay is faster. } ) ( 12. f4 { Avoids the complications of g4 and goes for a different pawn push. } 12... Qc7 {
Prepares ...Bc4 in case f5 comes. } 13. Nd5 ( 13. f5 Bc4 { Taking on c4 would leave the knight on b3 in a bad
way, so White retreats the bishop, and Black plays ...a5 and ...b4. } ) 13... Bxd5 14. exd5 O-O 15. g3 Rfe8 16.
h3 Ne4 { The knight comes with tempo on the queen. } 17. Qg2 exf4 18. gxf4 Bf6 { Equal: Black has ideas on
both wings. } ) 12... Bxd5 { With the bishop, not the knight: after ...Nxd5 the recapture exd5 would hit the bishop on e6 with tempo,
whereas now the knight on b6 puts more pressure on d5. } 13. exd5 Nb6 { [%cal Gb6d5,Gf6d5] Threatens
the d5 pawn, which cannot be defended by c4 because of the b5 pawn. } 14. Bxb6 Qxb6 15. Na5 { The only way for
White not to be worse: the knight heads for c6 and a quick ending. } 15... Rc8 16. Nc6 Nxd5 17. Nxe7 Nxe7 18.
Qxd6 Qxd6 19. Rxd6 Nc6 { **Verdict:** equal. Black has more space and active rooks, and the king comes to e7. }
*

[Line "English Attack: 9...h5 10.Nd5 and the exchange sacrifice on a3"]
[Section "6.Be3 e5: the ...h5 set-up"]
[Summary "White trades knights at once. Black recaptures with the knight, keeps the bishop pair and plays ...a5 and ...Nd7. The other recapture 10...Bxd5 allows a very different, more risky game with a sacrifice of the exchange."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. f3 Be6 9. Qd2 h5 10. Nd5 {
White heads for kingside castling and forces the issue on d5. } 10... Nxd5 { [%cal Ge6d5] If possible, Black
keeps the bishop pair. } ( 10... Bxd5!? { The other recapture is playable, with a different character. } 11.
exd5 Nbd7 12. O-O-O Qc7 13. c4 a5 14. Kb1 O-O 15. Na1 a4 16. Nc2 a3 17. Nxa3 Rxa3 18. bxa3 Ra8 { Black has
given up the exchange and a pawn for the open a-file and the white king. Objectively White is better, but it
is difficult to prove with the king on b1. } ) 11. exd5 Bf5 { [%cal Gf5c2] } 12. Be2 { White finishes
development. 12.Na5 does not work: after 12...b6 the knight cannot go to c6, because the b8 knight guards it. }
12... a5 { [%cal Ga5a4] Black stops Na5 for good, so that the b8 knight can come out. } 13. a4 { Stops ...a4, which would drive the knight from b3 to an awkward square. } 13... Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5] } 14. O-O O-O 15. f4 exf4 16. Bxf4 Bg6 17. Kh1
Qc7 { **Verdict:** equal. The d5 pawn is a target and the black bishops control the board, but White's
pieces are well placed and the position needs care. } *
